.Sh DESCRIPTION
In order to help protect applications (particularly long-running
servers) from pointer overruns or underruns that could return arbitrary
data from the program's dynamic memory area, where keys and other
sensitive information might be stored, OpenSSL supports the concept of a
"secure heap."
The level and type of security guarantees depend on the operating
system.
It is a good idea to review the code and see if it addresses your threat
model and concerns.
It should be noted that the secure heap uses a single read/write lock,
and therefore any operations that involve allocation or freeing of
secure heap memory are serialised, blocking other threads.
With that in mind, highly concurrent applications should enable the
secure heap with caution and be aware of the performance implications
for multi-threaded code.
.Pp
If a secure heap is used, then private key
.Sy BIGNUM
values are stored there.
This protects long-term storage of private keys, but will not
necessarily put all intermediate values and computations there.
.Pp
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc_init
creates the secure heap, with the specified
.Qo Li size Qc
in bytes.
The
.Qo Li minsize Qc
parameter is the minimum size to allocate from the heap or zero to use a
reasonable default value.
Both
.Qo Li size Qc
and, if specified,
.Qo Li minsize Qc
must be a power of two and
.Qo Li minsize Qc
should generally be small, for example 16 or 32.
.Qo Li minsize Qc
must be less than a quarter of
.Qo Li size Qc
in any case.
.Pp
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc_initialized
indicates whether or not the secure heap as been initialized and is
available.
.Pp
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc_done
releases the heap and makes the memory unavailable to the process if all
secure memory has been freed.
It can take noticeably long to complete.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_malloc
allocates
.Qo Li num Qc
bytes from the heap.
If
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc_init
is not called, this is equivalent to calling
.Xr OPENSSL_malloc 3 .
It is a macro that expands to
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc
and adds the
.Qo Li __FILE__ Qc
and
.Qo Li __LINE__ Qc
parameters.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_zalloc
and
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_zalloc
are like
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_malloc
and
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc ,
respectively, except that they call
.Xr memset 3
to zero the memory before returning.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_free
releases the memory at
.Qo Li ptr Qc
back to the heap.
It must be called with a value previously obtained from
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_malloc .
If
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc_init
is not called, this is equivalent to calling
.Xr OPENSSL_free 3 .
It exists for consistency with
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_malloc
, and is a macro that expands to
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_free
and adds the
.Qo Li __FILE__ Qc
and
.Qo Li __LINE__ Qc
parameters..
If the argument to
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_free
is NULL, nothing is done.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_clear_free
is similar to
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_free
except that it has an additional
.Qo Li num Qc
parameter which is used to clear the memory if it was not allocated from
the secure heap.
If
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc_init
is not called, this is equivalent to calling
.Xr OPENSSL_clear_free 3 .
If the argument to
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_clear_free
is NULL, nothing is done.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_actual_size
tells the actual size allocated to the pointer; implementations may
allocate more space than initially requested, in order to "round up" and
reduce secure heap fragmentation.
.Pp
.Xr OPENSSL_secure_allocated 3
tells if a pointer is allocated in the secure heap.
.Pp
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_used
returns the number of bytes allocated in the secure heap.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc_init
returns 0 on failure, 1 if successful, and 2 if successful but the heap
could not be protected by memory mapping.
.Pp
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc_initialized
returns 1 if the secure heap is available (that is, if
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc_init
has been called, but
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc_done
has not been called or failed) or 0 if not.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_malloc
and
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_zalloc
return a pointer into the secure heap of the requested size, or
.Qo Li NULL Qc
if memory could not be allocated.
.Pp
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_allocated
returns 1 if the pointer is in the secure heap, or 0 if not.
.Pp
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc_done
returns 1 if the secure memory area is released, or 0 if not.
.Pp
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_free
and
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_clear_free
return no values.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr OPENSSL_malloc 3 ,
.Xr BN_new 3
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn OPENSSL_secure_clear_free
function was added in OpenSSL 1.1.0g.
.Pp
The second argument to
.Fn CRYPTO_secure_malloc_init
was changed from an
.Vt int
to a
.Vt size_t
in OpenSSL 3.0.
